@namespace GroupSplit.App.Shared.Components

@*
    Rows that are not there yet.

    A list has three states and only two of them were ever drawn: full, and empty. "Not
    asked yet" looked exactly like "there is nothing here", so a screen whose read takes a
    moment announced an emptiness it had not established, then contradicted itself -- and
    the card grew as the real rows arrived under a heading that had already settled.

    Rows rather than a spinner because that is what is coming. A spinner says something is
    happening; these say what will be there and roughly how much of it, so the layout is
    already the right size when it arrives.
*@

@* gs-placeholder, not gs-waiting: the home page's "Waiting on you" card had that class
   first, and a second meaning for it landed later in the stylesheet at the same
   specificity, so the skeleton's padding won and stripped that card to its border --
   heading against one edge, count against the other. Two unrelated senses of "waiting",
   one class name. *@

<div class="gs-list gs-placeholder" aria-busy="true" aria-live="polite">
    <span class="gs-sr-only">Loading</span>

    @for (var row = 0; row < Rows; row++)
    {
        <div class="gs-placeholder-row" @key="row" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>How many rows to stand in for.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Guessed, and deliberately on the low side: a placeholder taller than what replaces
    /// it makes the page settle upwards, which reads as content being taken away.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public int Rows { get; set; } = 3;
}
